{"level":30,"time":1776978931058,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AEzghZHxJEKv4Lk2QqgCfQ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 31 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: diagram — North arrow and graphical scale for the drawing.\n     4→\n     5→NORTH\n     6→[North Arrow Symbol]\n     7→SCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")\n     8→0 10 20 50\n     9→\n    10→## Block 2: drawing — A detailed site plan drawing showing the layout of buildings, parking lots, landscaping areas, and other site features. The drawing includes contours, property lines, utility lines, and symbols corresponding to the key notes.\n    11→\n    12→This block contains a complex site plan drawing. It illustrates the layout of the proposed townhomes, parking areas, and surrounding landscaping. Key features include:\n    13→- Building footprints\n    14→- Parking lot with designated spaces and landscaping islands\n    15→- Sidewalks and pathways\n    16→- Trees and vegetation indicated by various symbols (e.g., circles for trees)\n    17→- Contour lines with elevation labels (e.g., 680, 681, 682)\n    18→- Utility lines such as water (W), storm drain (SD), and overhead electric (OHE)\n    19→- Fences marked with 'X' along the property lines\n    20→- Various key note symbols (e.g., H.1, H.2, H.3) referencing the legend\n    21→- Text labels indicating specific areas or features (e.g., 'Valley View Road', 'Existing Stone Retaining Wall')\n    22→- Coordinates and station numbers along the edges\n    23→\n    24→## Block 4: notes — General notes regarding landscape requirements, including finished elevations for parking lot islands and supplemental irrigation during drought stages.\n    25→\n    26→NOTE:\n    27→FINISHED ELEVATION FOR PARKING-LOT ISLANDS, MEDIANS, PENINSULAS, AND SIMILAR LANDSCAPE AREAS MUST BE AT LEAST SIX (6) INCHES BELOW THE FINISHED PARKING LOT ELEVATION TO ALLOW FOR PLACEMENT OF SIX (6) INCHES OF TOPSOIL [LDC 25-2-1007(F)(4)]\n    28→IF ESTABLISHING VEGETATION DURING ANY STAGE OF A DROUGHT, SECTION 6-4-30 MAY REQUIRE A VARIANCE. CONTACT AUSTIN WATER STAFF AT WATERUSECOMPVAR@AUSTINTEXAS.GOV OR CALL (512)974-2199.\n    29→SUPPLEMENTAL IRRIGATION IS REQUIRED FOR NEWLY PLANTED, REQUIRED LANDSCAPE PLANTS PER LDC 25-8-1008, ECM 2.4.6, AND ECM APPENDIX 0.\n    30→FOR PARKING-LOT ISLANDS, MEDIANS, PENINSULAS, AND SIMILAR LANDSCAPE AREAS THAT ARE TO RECEIVE PARKING LOT STORMWATER RUNOFF, FINISHED ELEVATION MUST BE AT LEAST SIX (6) INCHES BELOW THE FINISHED CURB ELEVATION TO ALLOW FOR PLACEMENT OF SIX (6) INCHES OF TOPSOIL [ECM 1.4.7].\n    31→\n    32→## Block 5: notes — Key notes for various landscape elements, including proposed paving, street yard, parking lot landscaping, common open spaces, and fences, with corresponding symbols.\n    33→\n    34→KEY NOTES:\n    35→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.1'] [Pattern: Stippled] PROPOSED PAVING\n    36→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.2'] [Pattern: Solid grey] STREET YARD\n    37→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.3'] [Pattern: Diagonal lines] STREET YARD LANDSCAPING\n    38→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.4'] [Pattern: Crosshatch] PARKING LOT LANDSCAPING\n    39→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.5'] [Pattern: Vertical lines] PRIVATE COMMON OPEN SPACE\n    40→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.6'] [Pattern: Line with 'X's] WOODEN PRIVACY FENCE\n    41→[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.7'] [Pattern: Wavy lines] SOD\n    42→SY - STREET YARD TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\n    43→PL - PARKING LOT TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\n    44→P - PARKING BUFFER POINT TREE\n    45→M - MITIGATION TREE\n    46→\n    47→## Block 7: text_block — Details of the project owner.\n    48→\n    49→OWNER:\n    50→WM 41 AUSTIN\n    51→RE, LLC\n    52→\n    53→## Block 8: text_block — Name of the project.\n    54→\n    55→PROJECT NAME:\n    56→VALLEY VIEW\n    57→TOWNHOMES\n    58→\n    59→## Block 9: text_block — Address of the project location.\n    60→\n    61→PROJECT LOCATION:\n    62→3759 VALLEY\n    63→VIEW ROAD,\n    64→AUSTIN,\n    65→TEXAS 78704\n    66→\n    67→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94b43057-6c8d-4501-ab55-21f141703f72","uuid":"8c4070ae-1be6-4323-a21f-6259b6925706","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-31/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 31 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — North arrow and graphical scale for the drawing.\n\nNORTH\n[North Arrow Symbol]\nSCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")\n0 10 20 50\n\n## Block 2: drawing — A detailed site plan drawing showing the layout of buildings, parking lots, landscaping areas, and other site features. The drawing includes contours, property lines, utility lines, and symbols corresponding to the key notes.\n\nThis block contains a complex site plan drawing. It illustrates the layout of the proposed townhomes, parking areas, and surrounding landscaping. Key features include:\n- Building footprints\n- Parking lot with designated spaces and landscaping islands\n- Sidewalks and pathways\n- Trees and vegetation indicated by various symbols (e.g., circles for trees)\n- Contour lines with elevation labels (e.g., 680, 681, 682)\n- Utility lines such as water (W), storm drain (SD), and overhead electric (OHE)\n- Fences marked with 'X' along the property lines\n- Various key note symbols (e.g., H.1, H.2, H.3) referencing the legend\n- Text labels indicating specific areas or features (e.g., 'Valley View Road', 'Existing Stone Retaining Wall')\n- Coordinates and station numbers along the edges\n\n## Block 4: notes — General notes regarding landscape requirements, including finished elevations for parking lot islands and supplemental irrigation during drought stages.\n\nNOTE:\nFINISHED ELEVATION FOR PARKING-LOT ISLANDS, MEDIANS, PENINSULAS, AND SIMILAR LANDSCAPE AREAS MUST BE AT LEAST SIX (6) INCHES BELOW THE FINISHED PARKING LOT ELEVATION TO ALLOW FOR PLACEMENT OF SIX (6) INCHES OF TOPSOIL [LDC 25-2-1007(F)(4)]\nIF ESTABLISHING VEGETATION DURING ANY STAGE OF A DROUGHT, SECTION 6-4-30 MAY REQUIRE A VARIANCE. CONTACT AUSTIN WATER STAFF AT WATERUSECOMPVAR@AUSTINTEXAS.GOV OR CALL (512)974-2199.\nSUPPLEMENTAL IRRIGATION IS REQUIRED FOR NEWLY PLANTED, REQUIRED LANDSCAPE PLANTS PER LDC 25-8-1008, ECM 2.4.6, AND ECM APPENDIX 0.\nFOR PARKING-LOT ISLANDS, MEDIANS, PENINSULAS, AND SIMILAR LANDSCAPE AREAS THAT ARE TO RECEIVE PARKING LOT STORMWATER RUNOFF, FINISHED ELEVATION MUST BE AT LEAST SIX (6) INCHES BELOW THE FINISHED CURB ELEVATION TO ALLOW FOR PLACEMENT OF SIX (6) INCHES OF TOPSOIL [ECM 1.4.7].\n\n## Block 5: notes — Key notes for various landscape elements, including proposed paving, street yard, parking lot landscaping, common open spaces, and fences, with corresponding symbols.\n\nKEY NOTES:\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.1'] [Pattern: Stippled] PROPOSED PAVING\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.2'] [Pattern: Solid grey] STREET YARD\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.3'] [Pattern: Diagonal lines] STREET YARD LANDSCAPING\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.4'] [Pattern: Crosshatch] PARKING LOT LANDSCAPING\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.5'] [Pattern: Vertical lines] PRIVATE COMMON OPEN SPACE\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.6'] [Pattern: Line with 'X's] WOODEN PRIVACY FENCE\n[Symbol: Hexagon with 'H.7'] [Pattern: Wavy lines] SOD\nSY - STREET YARD TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\nPL - PARKING LOT TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\nP - PARKING BUFFER POINT TREE\nM - MITIGATION TREE\n\n## Block 7: text_block — Details of the project owner.\n\nOWNER:\nWM 41 AUSTIN\nRE, LLC\n\n## Block 8: text_block — Name of the project.\n\nPROJECT NAME:\nVALLEY VIEW\nTOWNHOMES\n\n## Block 9: text_block — Address of the project location.\n\nPROJECT LOCATION:\n3759 VALLEY\nVIEW ROAD,\nAUSTIN,\nTEXAS 78704\n\n","numLines":67,"startLine":1,"totalLines":67}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776978931059,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LGAJGieign5fFTUbWHfVN3","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 30 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: drawing — This block is a detailed site plan showing the layout of multiple townhome buildings (Units 101 through 806), patio areas, and tree locations. It includes topographical lines, a concrete curb and gutter, and existing trees, some of which are marked to remain and others to be removed. Tree protection fencing is also indicated. The plan provides specific square footage and finished floor elevation (FFE) for each building.\n     4→\n     5→The image is a detailed site plan showing the layout of multiple townhome buildings, labeled as Units 101 through 806. The buildings are grouped into eight structures (Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8), each with a specific square footage and finished floor elevation (FFE) noted. \n     6→\n     7→For example:\n     8→- **Bldg. 1**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 688.50\n     9→- **Bldg. 2**: 4,830 sf, FFE = 685.50\n    10→- **Bldg. 3**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 688.0\n    11→- **Bldg. 4**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 685.0\n    12→- **Bldg. 5**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 683.0\n    13→- **Bldg. 6**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 683.0\n    14→- **Bldg. 7**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 685.0\n    15→- **Bldg. 8**: 4,997 sf, FFE = 685.50\n    16→\n    17→The plan also details the locations of existing trees, distinguishing between those to remain, those to be removed, and heritage trees to remain. Tree protection fencing is indicated around certain trees. Topographical contour lines traverse the site, indicating the terrain's elevation. A concrete curb and gutter are shown along the adjacent Valley View Road (60' R.O.W.). Specific notes indicate trees that are to remain but will be mitigated as if removed.\n    18→\n    19→## Block 2: notes — This section acts as a legend for the tree-related symbols used on the site plan. It clarifies the visual representation for tree protection fencing, existing heritage trees to remain, existing trees to remain, and existing trees to be removed.\n    20→\n    21→KEY NOTES:\n    22→\n    23→[Dashed line symbol] TREE PROTECTION FENCING - REF DETAIL 1/SHEET 6\n    24→\n    25→[Circle symbol with an 'H' in the center] EXISTING HERITAGE TREE - TO REMAIN\n    26→\n    27→[Circle symbol with a small dot in the center] EXISTING TREE - TO REMAIN\n    28→\n    29→[Circle symbol with an 'X' marking through it] EXISTING TREE - TO BE REMOVED\n    30→\n    31→NOTE: REFER SHEET 28 FOR TREE LIST.\n    32→\n    33→## Block 5: notes — This block outlines five specific construction techniques designed to protect existing trees during the excavation and construction process. The techniques cover cutting roots cleanly, protecting root zones from heavy equipment, removing protective materials post-construction, watering impacted trees, and using vapor barriers when installing concrete near tree roots.\n    34→\n    35→SPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\n    36→\n    37→1.  PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF TREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A CLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES WITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    38→2.  IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING CONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC IS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF ORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH SOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION 620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE MIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS PLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY ARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n    39→3.  ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE REMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3 INCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY HAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n    40→4.  WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION ACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE LEAVES.\n    41→5.  WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE, USE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT LEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n    42→\n    43→## Block 6: seal — This small section provides orientation and scale for the drawing. It includes a north arrow pointing upwards and a graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n    44→\n    45→NORTH\n    46→[North Arrow graphic]\n    47→\n    48→SCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")\n    49→[Graphic scale bar from 0 to 50]\n    50→0 10 20 50\n    51→\n    52→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94b43057-6c8d-4501-ab55-21f141703f72","uuid":"602e3867-37f0-4d8c-a660-73b497ec634f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-30/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 30 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: drawing — This block is a detailed site plan showing the layout of multiple townhome buildings (Units 101 through 806), patio areas, and tree locations. It includes topographical lines, a concrete curb and gutter, and existing trees, some of which are marked to remain and others to be removed. Tree protection fencing is also indicated. The plan provides specific square footage and finished floor elevation (FFE) for each building.\n\nThe image is a detailed site plan showing the layout of multiple townhome buildings, labeled as Units 101 through 806. The buildings are grouped into eight structures (Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8), each with a specific square footage and finished floor elevation (FFE) noted. \n\nFor example:\n- **Bldg. 1**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 688.50\n- **Bldg. 2**: 4,830 sf, FFE = 685.50\n- **Bldg. 3**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 688.0\n- **Bldg. 4**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 685.0\n- **Bldg. 5**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 683.0\n- **Bldg. 6**: 3,290 sf, FFE = 683.0\n- **Bldg. 7**: 4,144 sf, FFE = 685.0\n- **Bldg. 8**: 4,997 sf, FFE = 685.50\n\nThe plan also details the locations of existing trees, distinguishing between those to remain, those to be removed, and heritage trees to remain. Tree protection fencing is indicated around certain trees. Topographical contour lines traverse the site, indicating the terrain's elevation. A concrete curb and gutter are shown along the adjacent Valley View Road (60' R.O.W.). Specific notes indicate trees that are to remain but will be mitigated as if removed.\n\n## Block 2: notes — This section acts as a legend for the tree-related symbols used on the site plan. It clarifies the visual representation for tree protection fencing, existing heritage trees to remain, existing trees to remain, and existing trees to be removed.\n\nKEY NOTES:\n\n[Dashed line symbol] TREE PROTECTION FENCING - REF DETAIL 1/SHEET 6\n\n[Circle symbol with an 'H' in the center] EXISTING HERITAGE TREE - TO REMAIN\n\n[Circle symbol with a small dot in the center] EXISTING TREE - TO REMAIN\n\n[Circle symbol with an 'X' marking through it] EXISTING TREE - TO BE REMOVED\n\nNOTE: REFER SHEET 28 FOR TREE LIST.\n\n## Block 5: notes — This block outlines five specific construction techniques designed to protect existing trees during the excavation and construction process. The techniques cover cutting roots cleanly, protecting root zones from heavy equipment, removing protective materials post-construction, watering impacted trees, and using vapor barriers when installing concrete near tree roots.\n\nSPECIAL CONSTRUCTION TECHNIQUES\n\n1.  PRIOR TO EXCAVATION WITHIN TREE DRIP LINES OR THE REMOVAL OF TREES ADJACENT TO OTHER TREES THAT ARE TO REMAIN, MAKE A CLEAN CUT BETWEEN THE DISTURBED AND UNDISTURBED ROOT ZONES WITH A ROCK SAW OR SIMILAR EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n2.  IN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS THAT CANNOT BE PROTECTED DURING CONSTRUCTION WITH FENCING AND WHERE HEAVY VEHICULAR TRAFFIC IS ANTICIPATED, COVER THOSE AREAS WITH A MINIMUM OF 12 INCHES OF ORGANIC MULCH TO MINIMIZE SOIL COMPACTION. IN AREAS WITH HIGH SOIL PLASTICITY GEOTEXTILE FABRIC, PER STANDARD SPECIFICATION 620S, SHOULD BE PLACED UNDER THE MULCH TO PREVENT EXCESSIVE MIXING OF THE SOIL AND MULCH. ADDITIONALLY, MATERIAL SUCH AS PLYWOOD AND METAL SHEETS COULD BE REQUIRED BY THE CITY ARBORIST TO MINIMIZE ROOT IMPACTS FROM HEAVY EQUIPMENT.\n3.  ONCE THE PROJECT IS COMPLETED, ALL MATERIALS SHOULD BE REMOVED, AND THE MULCH SHOULD BE REDUCED TO A DEPTH OF 3 INCHES. PERFORM ALL GRADING WITHIN CRITICAL ROOT ZONE AREAS BY HAND OR WITH SMALL EQUIPMENT TO MINIMIZE ROOT DAMAGE.\n4.  WATER ALL TREES MOST HEAVILY IMPACTED BY CONSTRUCTION ACTIVITIES DEEPLY ONCE A WEEK DURING ACCUMULATION OF THE LEAVES.\n5.  WHEN INSTALLING CONCRETE ADJACENT TO THE ROOT ZONE OF A TREE, USE A PLASTIC VAPOR BARRIER BEHIND THE CONCRETE TO PROHIBIT LEACHING OF LIME INTO THE SOIL.\n\n## Block 6: seal — This small section provides orientation and scale for the drawing. It includes a north arrow pointing upwards and a graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n\nNORTH\n[North Arrow graphic]\n\nSCALE IN FEET (1\"=20'-0\")\n[Graphic scale bar from 0 to 50]\n0 10 20 50\n\n","numLines":52,"startLine":1,"totalLines":52}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776978931061,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CowH3V52ABrD1ktjaG2Wec","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 11 — Content Blocks\n     2→\n     3→## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n     4→\n     5→[graphic scale bar with increments 0, 20, 40]\n     6→Scale 1\" = 20'\n     7→\n     8→## Block 2: drawing — This is a detailed grading plan for a multi-family development. It shows existing and proposed contours, building footprints, retaining walls, storm drainage infrastructure, and various utility easements. Key features include multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each with finished floor elevations (FFE). The plan also indicates existing structures, vegetation, and adjacent property information.\n     9→\n    10→The plan displays a comprehensive layout for a multi-family development. It includes:\n    11→\n    12→*   **Building Footprints:** Eight buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, with their respective square footages and finished floor elevations (FFE). For example, Bldg. 1 is 3,290 sf with an FFE of 687.50, and Bldg. 8 is 4,997 sf with an FFE of 685.50.\n    13→*   **Topography:** Existing and proposed contour lines indicating the site's elevation changes.\n    14→*   **Infrastructure:** Proposed storm drain lines, area inlets, and concrete retaining walls.\n    15→*   **Easements:** Various easements are depicted, including drainage easements, wastewater easements, electric and telephone easements, and a greenbelt access and use easement.\n    16→*   **Adjacent Properties:** Information on adjacent properties, such as \"Lot A Cortex Heights Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-Family\" and \"William Lance Corsbie, Jr. 1.967 Acres Zoning: SF-3 Use: Single Family\".\n    17→*   **Existing Features:** Existing trees, rock rubble, and stone retaining walls.\n    18→*   **Utilities:** Locations of existing water manholes, gas meters, wastewater manholes, and AT&T vaults.\n    19→*   **Specific Details:** The plan includes specific details like a sediment basin, an existing retaining wall to be replaced, and a sidewalk easement.\n    20→\n    21→## Block 3: notes — Provides the legal description of the property being developed.\n    22→\n    23→Legal Description\n    24→Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n    25→\n    26→## Block 4: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for a list of trees.\n    27→\n    28→Tree List\n    29→Refer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n    30→\n    31→## Block 5: notes — This is the legend for the grading plan, explaining the symbols and line types used to represent various features on the drawing, such as contours, spot elevations, curbs, limits of construction, storm drains, level landings, and ADA ramps.\n    32→\n    33→Legend\n    34→[dashed line] --700-- Existing Contour\n    35→[solid line] --700-- Proposed Contour\n    36→[plus sign] 698.25 Proposed Spot Elevation\n    37→700.00\n    38→699.50 Top of Curb\n    39→Bottom of Curb\n    40→[solid line with LOC] Proposed Limits of Construction\n    41→[dashed line with SD] Proposed Storm Drain Line\n    42→L= Level Landing\n    43→(Not to exceed 2% in any Direction)\n    44→Down [arrow] ADA Ramp\n    45→(Not to exceed 8.33%)\n    46→\n    47→## Block 6: notes — A note providing specific instructions regarding the construction of grading lines related to curbs.\n    48→\n    49→Curb Note:\n    50→Grading lines that indicate flows toward the curb shall be constructed as a catch curb. Grade lines that indicate flows\n    51→away from curb shall be constructed as a spill curb.\n    52→\n    53→## Block 7: notes — Details two benchmark points used for elevation reference on the site plan.\n    54→\n    55→Benchmarks\n    56→B.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    57→Elevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    58→B.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\n    59→Elevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n    60→\n    61→## Block 11: text_block — A general disclaimer stating that the application does not constitute verification of all data, and the engineer of record is responsible for its completeness and accuracy.\n    62→\n    63→Release of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\n    64→applicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\n    65→submittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n    66→\n    67→## Block 13: text_block — A note advising that existing utility locations are based on record information and must be field-verified by the contractor prior to construction.\n    68→\n    69→The location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not\n    70→match locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility\n    71→locations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning\n    72→construction.\n    73→\n    74→## Block 14: text_block — A clear instruction to call before starting any excavation work.\n    75→\n    76→Call Before You Dig!!\n    77→\n    78→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94b43057-6c8d-4501-ab55-21f141703f72","uuid":"555d9b78-0e3b-435b-b2f2-44e84aa0df76","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-11/blocks.md","content":"# Sheet 11 — Content Blocks\n\n## Block 1: diagram — A graphic scale bar indicating that 1 inch equals 20 feet.\n\n[graphic scale bar with increments 0, 20, 40]\nScale 1\" = 20'\n\n## Block 2: drawing — This is a detailed grading plan for a multi-family development. It shows existing and proposed contours, building footprints, retaining walls, storm drainage infrastructure, and various utility easements. Key features include multiple buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, each with finished floor elevations (FFE). The plan also indicates existing structures, vegetation, and adjacent property information.\n\nThe plan displays a comprehensive layout for a multi-family development. It includes:\n\n*   **Building Footprints:** Eight buildings labeled Bldg. 1 through Bldg. 8, with their respective square footages and finished floor elevations (FFE). For example, Bldg. 1 is 3,290 sf with an FFE of 687.50, and Bldg. 8 is 4,997 sf with an FFE of 685.50.\n*   **Topography:** Existing and proposed contour lines indicating the site's elevation changes.\n*   **Infrastructure:** Proposed storm drain lines, area inlets, and concrete retaining walls.\n*   **Easements:** Various easements are depicted, including drainage easements, wastewater easements, electric and telephone easements, and a greenbelt access and use easement.\n*   **Adjacent Properties:** Information on adjacent properties, such as \"Lot A Cortex Heights Zoning: MF 3-CO Use: Multi-Family\" and \"William Lance Corsbie, Jr. 1.967 Acres Zoning: SF-3 Use: Single Family\".\n*   **Existing Features:** Existing trees, rock rubble, and stone retaining walls.\n*   **Utilities:** Locations of existing water manholes, gas meters, wastewater manholes, and AT&T vaults.\n*   **Specific Details:** The plan includes specific details like a sediment basin, an existing retaining wall to be replaced, and a sidewalk easement.\n\n## Block 3: notes — Provides the legal description of the property being developed.\n\nLegal Description\nLot 1, Mecey Subdivision Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas.\n\n## Block 4: notes — A brief note directing the reader to another sheet for a list of trees.\n\nTree List\nRefer to landscape plan sheet 32 for the tree list.\n\n## Block 5: notes — This is the legend for the grading plan, explaining the symbols and line types used to represent various features on the drawing, such as contours, spot elevations, curbs, limits of construction, storm drains, level landings, and ADA ramps.\n\nLegend\n[dashed line] --700-- Existing Contour\n[solid line] --700-- Proposed Contour\n[plus sign] 698.25 Proposed Spot Elevation\n700.00\n699.50 Top of Curb\nBottom of Curb\n[solid line with LOC] Proposed Limits of Construction\n[dashed line with SD] Proposed Storm Drain Line\nL= Level Landing\n(Not to exceed 2% in any Direction)\nDown [arrow] ADA Ramp\n(Not to exceed 8.33%)\n\n## Block 6: notes — A note providing specific instructions regarding the construction of grading lines related to curbs.\n\nCurb Note:\nGrading lines that indicate flows toward the curb shall be constructed as a catch curb. Grade lines that indicate flows\naway from curb shall be constructed as a spill curb.\n\n## Block 7: notes — Details two benchmark points used for elevation reference on the site plan.\n\nBenchmarks\nB.M. #1 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 678.52' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\nB.M. #2 - Mag Nail Set in Asphalt\nElevation = 681.08' NAVD 88 (GEOID 18)\n\n## Block 11: text_block — A general disclaimer stating that the application does not constitute verification of all data, and the engineer of record is responsible for its completeness and accuracy.\n\nRelease of this application does not constitute a verification of all data, information and calculations supplied by the\napplicant. The engineer of record is solely responsible for the completeness, accuracy and adequacy of his/her\nsubmittal, whether or not the application is reviewed for Code compliance by City engineers.\n\n## Block 13: text_block — A note advising that existing utility locations are based on record information and must be field-verified by the contractor prior to construction.\n\nThe location of all existing utilities shown on these plans has been based upon record information only and may not\nmatch locations as constructed. The contractor shall contact Texas 811 for assistance in determining existing utility\nlocations prior to beginning construction. Contractor shall field verify locations of utility crossings prior to beginning\nconstruction.\n\n## Block 14: text_block — A clear instruction to call before starting any excavation work.\n\nCall Before You Dig!!\n\n","numLines":78,"startLine":1,"totalLines":78}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776978931149,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":3,"prompt":"Please examine Sheet 3 and identify all notes, text blocks, or requirements related to electric transmission lines, transmission easements, transmission clearances, or Austin Energy transmission facilities. Provide the exact text of any transmission-related requirements, including any specifications for clearances, setbacks, structural requirements, tree restrictions, or easement management. Also note any references to transmission structures, poles, conductors, or high-voltage facilities.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on Sheet 3 of the site plan document, there are extensive requirements and notes pertaining to electric transmission lines, easements, clearances, and Austin Energy facilities. \n\nThese requirements are primarily found in two sections: **Electric Transmission Notes** (located in the upper-middle section of the page) and **Electric Notes** (located in the second column, with identical unnumbered duplicate text also appearing below the Electric Transmission Notes).\n\nHere is the exact character-by-character transcription of the relevant notes:\n\n### **Electric Transmission Notes**\n\"1. Trees shall not be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. **Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors.** Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (alt 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24 hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust control to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be drivable/rollable.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures.\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures (including down guy anchors).\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    * any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices.\n    * any structure of any kind in such proximity to transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    * any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\"\n\n### **Electric Notes**\n*(Note: These 5 points appear numbered under the \"Electric Notes\" header in the second column, and identical unnumbered paragraphs are also duplicated directly below the \"Electric Transmission Notes\".)*\n\n\"1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\"\n\n### **Summary of Specific Restrictions & Requirements Extracted:**\n*   **Clearance & Setbacks:**\n    *   **OSHA:** 20-foot clearance required from energized lines during construction (Note 3).\n    *   **Digging/Grading:** Prohibited within 25 feet of structures/down guys (Notes 2 & 15).\n    *   **Barricades:** Must be 10 feet from structures during construction (Note 4).\n    *   **Staging/Dumpsters:** Prohibited within 20 feet of wires/structures and must be outside the easement entirely (Note 7).\n    *   **Fire Hydrants:** Must be outside the easement and minimum 20 feet from any structure (Note 13).\n    *   **Sprinklers:** Prohibited within 25 feet of poles/structures (Note 14).\n*   **Tree/Vegetation Restrictions:**\n    *   No trees allowed within the transmission easement (Note 1).\n    *   No trees planted within 25 feet of the base of the structure (Note 1).\n    *   Initial pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of overhead facilities is the owner's responsibility (Electric Note 3).\n*   **Structural & Easement Constraints:**\n    *   **Pavement/Roads:** Must support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (Note 12). Curbs must be drivable/rollable (Note 11).\n    *   **Access:** 24-hour access must be maintained. Temporary/permanent fences require coordination and an AE staff lock (Notes 8 & 9).\n    *   **Prohibited Structures:** No permanent/habitable structures, fences, sheds, or detention ponds that impair access can be placed within the easement (Note 16).","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1391,"outputTokens":1641,"totalTokens":7613,"reasoningTokens":4581},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 3237656]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Please examine Sheet 3 and identify all notes, text blocks, or requirements related to electric transmission lines, transmission easements, transmission clearances, or Austin Energy transmission facilities. Provide the exact text of any transmission-related requirements, including any specifications for clearances, setbacks, structural requirements, tree restrictions, or easement management. Also note any references to transmission structures, poles, conductors, or high-voltage facilities."}]}],"headers":{"user-agent":"ai/5.0.152"}}},"response":{"id":"aitxt-MzrktejfMyepFTzQXDgVGTe8","timestamp":{},"modelId":"google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview","headers":{"access-control-allow-headers":"Content-Type, Authorization, ai-gateway-auth-method, ai-gateway-protocol-version, ai-model-id, ai-language-model-id, ai-language-model-specification-version, ai-image-model-specification-version, ai-embedding-model-specification-version, ai-language-model-streaming, ai-reporting-tags, ai-reporting-user, http-referer, x-title, anthropic-beta","access-control-allow-methods":"GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE, OPTIONS","access-control-allow-origin":"*","cache-control":"public, max-age=0, must-revalidate","content-encoding":"br","content-type":"application/json","date":"Thu, 23 Apr 2026 21:15:31 GMT","server":"Vercel","strict-transport-security":"max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload","transfer-encoding":"chunked","vary":"rsc, next-router-state-tree, next-router-prefetch, next-router-segment-prefetch","x-matched-path":"/v1/ai/language-model","x-vercel-cache":"MISS","x-vercel-id":"iad1::iad1::svfh2-1776978885271-216dfbeaf381"},"body":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on Sheet 3 of the site plan document, there are extensive requirements and notes pertaining to electric transmission lines, easements, clearances, and Austin Energy facilities. \n\nThese requirements are primarily found in two sections: **Electric Transmission Notes** (located in the upper-middle section of the page) and **Electric Notes** (located in the second column, with identical unnumbered duplicate text also appearing below the Electric Transmission Notes).\n\nHere is the exact character-by-character transcription of the relevant notes:\n\n### **Electric Transmission Notes**\n\"1. Trees shall not be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. **Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors.** Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512 505 7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (alt 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24 hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust control to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be drivable/rollable.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures.\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures (including down guy anchors).\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    * any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices.\n    * any structure of any kind in such proximity to transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    * any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\"\n\n### **Electric Notes**\n*(Note: These 5 points appear numbered under the \"Electric Notes\" header in the second column, and identical unnumbered paragraphs are also duplicated directly below the \"Electric Transmission Notes\".)*\n\n\"1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\"\n\n### **Summary of Specific Restrictions & Requirements Extracted:**\n*   **Clearance & Setbacks:**\n    *   **OSHA:** 20-foot clearance required from energized lines during construction (Note 3).\n    *   **Digging/Grading:** Prohibited within 25 feet of structures/down guys (Notes 2 & 15).\n    *   **Barricades:** Must be 10 feet from structures during construction (Note 4).\n    *   **Staging/Dumpsters:** Prohibited within 20 feet of wires/structures and must be outside the easement entirely (Note 7).\n    *   **Fire Hydrants:** Must be outside the easement and minimum 20 feet from any structure (Note 13).\n    *   **Sprinklers:** Prohibited within 25 feet of poles/structures (Note 14).\n*   **Tree/Vegetation Restrictions:**\n    *   No trees allowed within the transmission easement (Note 1).\n    *   No trees planted within 25 feet of the base of the structure (Note 1).\n    *   Initial pruning/removal within 10 feet of the centerline of overhead facilities is the owner's responsibility (Electric Note 3).\n*   **Structural & Easement Constraints:**\n    *   **Pavement/Roads:** Must support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (Note 12). Curbs must be drivable/rollable (Note 11).\n    *   **Access:** 24-hour access must be maintained. Temporary/permanent fences require coordination and an AE staff lock (Notes 8 & 9).\n    *   **Prohibited Structures:** No permanent/habitable structures, fences, sheds, or detention ponds that impair access can be placed within the easement (Note 16).","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1391,"outputTokens":1641,"totalTokens":7613,"reasoningTokens":4581},"warnings":[],"providerMetadata":{"google":{"promptFeedback":null,"groundingMetadata":null,"urlContextMetadata":null,"safetyRatings":null,"serviceTier":null},"gateway":{"routing":{"originalModelId":"google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview","resolvedProvider":"vertex