{"level":30,"time":1776979436961,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTgqaMjQdJwNkZaDQrnExF","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 14\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacity for the proposed Valley View Townhomes project.\n     4→\n     5→AW\n     6→City of Austin | Austin Water\n     7→6310 Wilhelmina Delco Drive, Suite 3100\n     8→Austin, Texas 78752\n     9→http://www.austintexas.gov/SER\n    10→SER@austintexas.gov\n    11→\n    12→Mr. Jason Rodgers, P.E.\n    13→Bleyl Engineering\n    14→7701 San Felipe Blvd Ste 200\n    15→Austin, Texas 78729\n    16→\n    17→Re: Letter of Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\n    18→3759 Valley View Townhomes Service Extension Request for Wastewater (SER-6325)\n    19→3759 Valley View, Austin, Texas 78704\n    20→\n    21→Dear Mr. Rodgers:\n    22→\n    23→Austin Water has reviewed your submittal for the above referenced Service Extension Request (SER) project and is issuing this letter of Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service in keeping with all current rules and regulations.\n    24→\n    25→Submitted project specifications:\n    26→39 Condo Units (6 to 24 Units/Acre)\n    27→Living Unit Equivalent (LUE): 28\n    28→Subject Tract Area: 3.59 Acres\n    29→Wastewater Flow (Estimated Peak Wet Weather): 20 gpm\n    30→Wastewater flow submitted in the engineering calculations received from Jason Rodgers, P.E. of Bleyl Engineering on 10/20/2025.\n    31→\n    32→Based on the submitted project specifications and the results of the Service Extension Request review, it has been determined that the existing 8-inch gravity wastewater main (MH No. 15466) in Valley View Rd. is suitable and sufficient to provide service to the subject tract.\n    33→\n    34→Due to the existing suitable and sufficient wastewater main at the subject tract, a wastewater main extension/improvement is not required for this project.\n    35→\n    36→Wastewater service for this project can be provided based upon plan approval within the Austin Water plan review process. It is suggested that this letter be submitted with your site development plans.\n    37→\n    38→This letter will automatically expire 180 days after its date of issue unless a development application has been accepted by the Development Services Department (DSD). If a development application has been accepted by DSD, then this letter expires on the date the development application, or its approval, expires.\n    39→\n    40→If we can provide additional information, please call me at (512) 972-0035.\n    41→\n    42→Sincerely,\n    43→[Signature]\n    44→Christine Perez, P.E.\n    45→Austin Water\n    46→Utility Development Services Division\n    47→\n    48→DATE: 12/10/2025\n    49→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11ce902f-86c5-4003-b16c-9143da511761","uuid":"d7bc7a35-cba1-4be4-9506-f1028efda46f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-14.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 14\n\n## text_block: Copy of a letter from Austin Water confirming that the existing wastewater main has adequate capacity for the proposed Valley View Townhomes project.\n\nAW\nCity of Austin | Austin Water\n6310 Wilhelmina Delco Drive, Suite 3100\nAustin, Texas 78752\nhttp://www.austintexas.gov/SER\nSER@austintexas.gov\n\nMr. Jason Rodgers, P.E.\nBleyl Engineering\n7701 San Felipe Blvd Ste 200\nAustin, Texas 78729\n\nRe: Letter of Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service\n3759 Valley View Townhomes Service Extension Request for Wastewater (SER-6325)\n3759 Valley View, Austin, Texas 78704\n\nDear Mr. Rodgers:\n\nAustin Water has reviewed your submittal for the above referenced Service Extension Request (SER) project and is issuing this letter of Suitable and Sufficient Existing Service in keeping with all current rules and regulations.\n\nSubmitted project specifications:\n39 Condo Units (6 to 24 Units/Acre)\nLiving Unit Equivalent (LUE): 28\nSubject Tract Area: 3.59 Acres\nWastewater Flow (Estimated Peak Wet Weather): 20 gpm\nWastewater flow submitted in the engineering calculations received from Jason Rodgers, P.E. of Bleyl Engineering on 10/20/2025.\n\nBased on the submitted project specifications and the results of the Service Extension Request review, it has been determined that the existing 8-inch gravity wastewater main (MH No. 15466) in Valley View Rd. is suitable and sufficient to provide service to the subject tract.\n\nDue to the existing suitable and sufficient wastewater main at the subject tract, a wastewater main extension/improvement is not required for this project.\n\nWastewater service for this project can be provided based upon plan approval within the Austin Water plan review process. It is suggested that this letter be submitted with your site development plans.\n\nThis letter will automatically expire 180 days after its date of issue unless a development application has been accepted by the Development Services Department (DSD). If a development application has been accepted by DSD, then this letter expires on the date the development application, or its approval, expires.\n\nIf we can provide additional information, please call me at (512) 972-0035.\n\nSincerely,\n[Signature]\nChristine Perez, P.E.\nAustin Water\nUtility Development Services Division\n\nDATE: 12/10/2025\n","numLines":49,"startLine":1,"totalLines":49}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776979436961,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017fRTGV18KeuDijTLHbCBA3","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Transmission Notes\n     6→1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n     7→2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n     8→3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n     9→4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    11→6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n    12→7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n    13→8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n    14→9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n    15→10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n    16→11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n    17→12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n    18→13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n    19→14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n    20→15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n    21→16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    22→  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n    23→  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n    24→  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n    25→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11ce902f-86c5-4003-b16c-9143da511761","uuid":"5d9a1dcc-9298-4a9b-8d55-95ad840fb10f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-13.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 13\n\n## text_block: List of 16 notes detailing restrictions and requirements for work near electric transmission lines and easements, including clearance zones, safety meetings, access, barricades, and prohibited structures.\n\nElectric Transmission Notes\n1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\n2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\n3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (ph 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\n4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\n5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\n7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\n8. 24-hour access to electric facilities shall be maintained.\n9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\n10. Property owner and contractor are responsible for dust controls to minimize contamination of wire and insulators caused by dust from this project. Any subsequent cleaning or electrical outages caused by dust from this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\n11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structures/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be laydown curbing.\n12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\n13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\n14. Sprinklers are prohibited within 25 feet of transmission poles and structures\n15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors)\n16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n  · any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices,\n  · any structure of any kind in such proximity to the electric transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected, nor\n  · any structures, including but not limited to, fences, storage sheds, drainage, filtration or detention ponds which would impair Austin Energy's access to the transmission easements or its lines, poles, structures, towers or appurtenant facilities in the easements.\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776979436961,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013SXfEZjWiEipY3DM39LFrj","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n     2→\n     3→## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n     4→\n     5→Electric Notes\n     6→1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     7→2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n     8→3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n     9→4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n    10→5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n    11→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11ce902f-86c5-4003-b16c-9143da511761","uuid":"3594e0c1-5476-4bb6-8cd0-974df7eb8a8d","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/primary-site-plan/sheet-03/block-19.md","content":"# Sheet 3 — Block 19\n\n## text_block: List of 5 notes regarding electric utilities, specifically referencing Austin Energy's requirements for tree pruning, easements, clearances, and relocation costs.\n\nElectric Notes\n1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\n3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy's work within the limits of construction for this project.\n4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless require clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner's/developer's expense.\n","numLines":11,"startLine":1,"totalLines":11}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776979436962,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NuhHcLyWdN48F1GcSdTKFV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! Now I have very important information:\n\n**Block 13 - Electric Transmission Notes:**\nThese are critical. The notes address:\n1. No trees in transmission easement or within 25 feet of transmission structures\n2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of transmission structures\n3. 48-hour pre-construction safety meeting required with Austin Energy, OSHA requires 20' clearance from energized transmission lines during construction\n4. Barricades 10 feet from transmission structures\n5. 20-foot clearance for aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging\n6. Dust control requirements\n7. Fire hydrants minimum 20 feet from transmission structures\n8. Roads/driveways/pavement should support 46,000 lb tandem axle\n9. Owner may not place permanent structures within transmission easement or that would violate NESC\n\n**Block 19 - Electric Notes (Distribution, not transmission):**\nThese are about the overhead and underground electric distribution facilities (for service to the building):\n1. Austin Energy has right to prune/remove trees\n2. Owner/developer shall provide easement/access for electric service\n3. Owner responsible for tree pruning within 10 feet of centerline of proposed overhead electric facilities\n4. Owner responsible for maintaining clearances required by NESC, OSHA, COA, and Texas law\n5. Any relocation of electric facilities at owner's expense\n\nNow I need to determine: Are the existing overhead lines shown on Sheet 4 part of the transmission facility or distribution facility? Looking back at the facts.md and the vision analysis:\n- The existing overhead lines are labeled \"O.H.U.L.\" (overhead utility lines)\n- They run along the southern boundary in a 10' electric easement\n- The grouping document says distribution facilities operate at <60kV and transmission facilities operate at ≥60kV\n- The facts.md doesn't specify if this is transmission or distribution\n\n\n- The distinction matters because Block 13 governs transmission (≥60kV) while Block 19 governs distribution lines serving the building. I need to check the site plan and Sheet 38-39 for any voltage specifications or clearance dimensions that might clarify whether these existing lines are transmission or distribution infrastructure.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2760,"cache_read_input_tokens":58693,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2760,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"11ce902f-86c5-4003-b16c-9143da511761","uuid":"798fc608-a00a-4666-9373-6afd09eb2877"}
