{"level":30,"time":1776979821718,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","tool":"run_measure_distance","command":"npx tsx /vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/workflows/review/scripts/measure-distance.ts --outputPath='/vercel/sandbox/workspace/output/measure-distance.json' --reasoning='The landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows an overhead electric (OHE) line running along the southern edge of the property. The vision analysis identified multiple proposed tree symbols positioned directly in the path of or very close to the OHE line in the southeastern section. According to UCM § 1.10.10.4, non-utility-compatible trees must be at least 15 lateral feet from distribution conductors and large trees (40+ ft mature height) must be at least 25 feet away. I need to measure the actual distances from the outside conductor to the proposed trees to determine if clearances are violated. These measurements will apply to checklist items EL-2.1 (non-utility-compatible trees within 15 ft), EL-2.3 (large trees within 25 ft), and EL-2.12/EL-2.13 (measurement methodology).' --applicable_checklist_items='[{\"checklist_id\":\"EL-2.1\",\"checklist_deficiency_text\":\"Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment\"},{\"checklist_id\":\"EL-2.3\",\"checklist_deficiency_text\":\"Large trees (mature height 40+ feet per ECM Appendix F) proposed within 25 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor or equipment\"},{\"checklist_id\":\"EL-2.6\",\"checklist_deficiency_text\":\"Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of overhead electric utility pole\"}]' --documentId='1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777' --sheetNum='31' --objectPairs='[{\"objectA\":\"overhead electric (OHE) line conductor at southern property boundary in southeast section of site\",\"objectB\":\"proposed tree symbol (appears to be mitigation tree '\\''M'\\'') positioned under or very near OHE line in southeastern area\"},{\"objectA\":\"overhead electric (OHE) line conductor at southern property boundary\",\"objectB\":\"row of proposed trees along southern boundary near southeast corner\"}]' --scaleInchesPerFoot='0.05'","msg":"Executing typed script tool"}
{"level":30,"time":1776979988771,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"13.md","index":8,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01PULehCaDJs2jgds4Q3V9LG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is very helpful. I can see that the Electric Review section lists several UCM sections that are relevant to transformer pads, specifically:\n- UCM § 1.10.4 - Clearances from AE Padmount Equipment and Distribution Vaults\n- UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 - Customer Activity in Utility Easements - Landscaping & Trees\n- Appendix F of the Environmental Criteria Manual (for utility-compatible trees)\n\nHowever, I cannot access the Bluebeam link to see the actual Austin Energy Design review comments. The site plan is showing \"Preliminary Electrical Design Plan\" on Sheet 21, which suggests that Austin Energy Design has not yet provided detailed approvals.\n\nLet me now look at what I've found so far and evaluate the checklist items:\n\n## Research Summary - What I Found:\n\n### Evidence Found:\n1. **5 Transformer Pads Identified** on Sheet 21 (Electrical Design Plan):\n   - Pad 1: Northwest quadrant, west of Building 1\n   - Pad 2: Western edge, west of Building 2\n   - Pad 3: Southwest quadrant, west of Building 8\n   - Pad 4: Central area, east of Building 4, adjacent to parking\n   - Pad 5: South-central area, east of Building 7, adjacent to parking\n\n2. **NO Specific Dimensions Shown** on Sheet 21 for transformer pads or clearances\n\n3. **NO Detail Sheets Referenced** for transformer pads or AE Design-approved specifications\n\n4. **NO Austin Energy Design Approvals** shown on the plans (labeled \"Preliminary\")\n\n5. **Trees and Vegetation** shown on Landscape Plan (Sheet 31) near transformer areas, but specific distances and species not provided\n\n6. **NO Root Barrier Details** shown on landscape plan\n\n7. **NO Cross-Section Drawings** showing vertical clearances for transformer pads\n\n\n8. Sheet 3 references Austin Energy requirements but lacks specific transformer pad details.\n\n### Not Found / Could Not Verify:\nThe transformer pad dimensions, service type specifications (single-phase vs. three-phase), equipment type (oil-filled or dry), access door orientations, and horizontal clearances to buildings and parking areas are all absent from the plans. Bollard details and specifications aren't shown despite pads being positioned near parking, and detailed clearance requirements remain unspecified.\n\nUnderground utility clearances, overhead distribution line clearances, drainage patterns around oil-filled equipment, and easement specifications for the transformer pads are not documented. Cross-section drawings that would show vertical clearances are missing, as are fire ratings for transformer pads that might allow reduced clearances.\n\nThe landscape plan doesn't identify utility-compatible tree species or provide a complete species list beyond key note designations (SY, PL, P, M). Root barrier installation details are absent, and Austin Energy design approval documents aren't included in the site plan package.\n\nNow I'm evaluating each checklist item to determine whether requirements are met, deficiencies exist, evidence is insufficient, or the item doesn't apply. For EL-13.1 regarding transformer pad clearances from buildings and structures, the plans show pads positioned away from buildings but lack dimensioned callouts confirming the 5-foot minimum. This makes it not-verifiable since no specific distances are noted. For EL-13.2 on clearances from retaining walls, I need to examine Sheet 38/39 for wall locations and Sheet 21 for pad placement, but again no dimension lines connect them. EL-13.3 addresses fence clearances with different requirements for operator versus non-operator sides—fence locations appear on site plans but clearance dimensions and access door orientation are missing from documentation. Finally, EL-13.4 only becomes relevant if reduced 3-foot clearances actually exist on the plans; since none are shown, this item is either not-applicable or cannot be verified.\n\nBuilding sections would clarify whether pads maintain adequate lateral clearance from balconies or overhangs, but these elevations weren't thoroughly reviewed. Equipment specifications matter for the 12-foot horizontal clearance requirement from windows and doors—the plans don't identify whether transformers are oil-filled or dry-type, nor do they dimension these distances. Sidewalk proximity also lacks dimensional verification on the site plan.\n\nStairwell locations appear on building plans but lack dimension lines to the pads. For fire escape clearances, I need to know the equipment type and see dimensioned distances, neither of which are provided. The access door orientation isn't shown anywhere, making it impossible to verify the 10-foot hot-stick clearance requirement—this appears to be a compliance failure. Confined space clearance for the access door also needs verification.\n\nNo niche or enclosed area configuration is identified, and cross-sections aren't provided to assess the 15-foot clearance from confined spaces. Fire hydrant locations appear on Sheet 10, but there are no dimension lines connecting them to the pads. The landscape plan uses only abbreviations for tree types rather than species names, and there's no reference to ECM Appendix F to determine which trees are utility-compatible. Shrub placement also needs verification against the 10-foot access requirement.\n\nRoot barriers aren't detailed anywhere on the plans, and tree species remain unidentified. Dumpster and bike rack locations are shown, but dimensions to the pads are missing. Light pole clearances can't be verified since the photometric plan wasn't provided.\n\nFire lanes appear on Sheet 10 with no dimensional verification to the pads. The five transformer pads on Sheet 21 lack any spacing dimensions between them. Water, wastewater, and storm drain lines are documented on the utility plan, but none have clearance dimensions marked to the pad locations.\n\nGas line pressure classification isn't specified on the utility plan, making the required clearance distance indeterminate. Multiple utilities cross on Sheet 21 without documented 12-inch horizontal and vertical separations. Sheet 21 also shows utilities and potential building structures positioned relative to the AE facilities, but there's no clear documentation of whether any overlap or conflict exists.\n\nOverhead electric lines appear on Sheets 21 and 31, though the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearance from distribution conductors isn't dimensioned. The overhead lines lack identification as primary, neutral, or secondary conductors, and no radius clearances are shown around them. Niche installations don't show the required 35-foot vertical clearance on the plans.\n\nAll transformer pads appear to be exterior installations rather than niche configurations. Since no niche installation is identified, the requirements for 3-hour fire wall rating and ventilation documentation don't apply. Cross-section drawings are missing entirely for the transformer pad locations, which fails to demonstrate vertical clearances. The grading plan on Sheet 11 shows general site slopes but doesn't specify drainage direction around the transformer pads, and the equipment type (oil-filled versus dry) isn't identified on the plans.\n\nRegarding property boundaries and easements: all pads are positioned within private property limits per Sheet 21, which satisfies the private property requirement. However, while the pads visually appear to avoid the easements shown on Sheets 9 and 2, precise confirmation would need detailed measurement. Most critically, no transformer easements are shown or dimensioned anywhere on the plans. Electrical routing requirements are next to evaluate.\n\nFor the electrical routing and pad specifications: Sheet 21 indicates routing on private property, though explicit confirmation is lacking. More significantly, no pad dimensions or reference to AE Design-approved details appear anywhere on the plans, and pads 4 and 5 lack the required 4-inch bollard details despite their proximity to parking areas.\n\nRegarding the building location relative to overhead distribution lines, there's no documented conflict analysis showing whether structures pass beneath any lines. I'm also reconsidering my approach to these evaluations—the applicant bears responsibility for demonstrating compliance through plan documentation. When required information is simply absent rather than present but non-compliant, that typically warrants a fail status rather than marking it unverifiable. The distinction hinges on whether the plans affirmatively show compliance or whether they're missing the documentation needed to prove it.\n\nLooking at the clearance requirements more critically, several items lack the dimensional evidence needed: no clearance dimensions are marked for buildings, retaining walls, or fences; equipment specifications aren't identified; access door orientations aren't shown; and no detail sheets are referenced. This means items like horizontal clearances to structures, fence clearances with access door details, equipment type identification, and sidewalk setback dimensions should be marked as failures due to missing documentation rather than ambiguous evidence. Some items like balcony clearances might remain unverifiable if building elevations aren't provided, but the core dimensional requirements that should appear on the electrical plans are simply not there. The distinction between FAIL and not-verifiable hinges on whether the applicant bears responsibility for providing documentation. Missing required dimensions, specifications, detail sheets, and approvals constitute failures—the applicant must supply these. For clearance-based items, I should measure distances directly from the plans rather than defaulting to not-verifiable, since the instructions explicitly call for using the measure-distance tool to verify actual spacing where dimensions aren't shown.\n\nMost clearance requirements can be measured: distances from transformer pads to buildings (EL-13.1), retaining walls (EL-13.2), fences (EL-13.3), sidewalks (EL-13.7), fire hydrants (EL-13.12), dumpsters (EL-13.16), bike racks (EL-13.17), light poles (EL-13.18), and other adjacent features. I'll systematically measure each one to determine compliance rather than marking them as unverifiable. to understand all the features shown. I should check the grading plan on Sheet 11 and utility plan on Sheet 19 to get a complete picture of what's depicted.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":643,"cache_read_input_tokens":65401,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":643,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0ccf6950-9428-47a1-a0e1-f1b6954e2e70","uuid":"9e7b20ac-eba5-485e-bb16-6db5a6397966"}
