{"level":30,"time":1776980214077,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01KvdfAgoLMB85cdeFWkYBTY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\nI have completed the comprehensive review of **Grouping 2: Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines** for the Valley View Townhomes site plan.\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**Critical Violations Identified:**\n\n1. **Distance Measurements** (High-confidence): Proposed trees measured at **0.5 feet and 4 feet** from the overhead electric (OHE) distribution conductor along the southern property boundary, compared to required clearances of 15 feet (non-utility-compatible) to 25 feet (large trees).\n\n2. **Large Trees in Restricted Zones**: Cedar Elm (10 proposed; 50-60 ft mature) and Live Oak (2 proposed; 50-80 ft mature) are proposed directly adjacent to the OHE line, violating the 25-foot clearance requirement for large trees.\n\n3. **No Austin Energy Approval**: No written approval from Austin Energy authorizing trees within restricted clearance zones was found in project documents. Austin Energy review of tree/landscape clearances is listed as \"Pending\" in the Master Comment Report.\n\n4. **Incomplete Clearance Documentation**: The landscape plan shows no dimension callouts, distance notes, or measurement methodology documentation for overhead electric clearances.\n\n### Findings Provided\n\n- **3 FAIL items** (EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.15): Tree placement violations\n- **6 NOT-VERIFIABLE items** (EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, EL-2.9, EL-2.11, EL-2.12, EL-2.13): Required information missing from plans\n\nAll findings have been documented with specific code citations, applicable areas, sheet references, and detailed deficiency descriptions as required."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":9,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4985,"cache_read_input_tokens":66543,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4985,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6fe0ce48-478a-49fe-85e9-e6a74537d9e2","uuid":"df497f2d-ceb8-45be-9404-5260fa87695a"}
{"level":30,"time":1776980214077,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":1228306,"duration_api_ms":146601,"num_turns":21,"result":"## Summary\n\nI have completed the comprehensive review of **Grouping 2: Tree Clearances from Overhead Electric Lines** for the Valley View Townhomes site plan.\n\n### Key Findings\n\n**Critical Violations Identified:**\n\n1. **Distance Measurements** (High-confidence): Proposed trees measured at **0.5 feet and 4 feet** from the overhead electric (OHE) distribution conductor along the southern property boundary, compared to required clearances of 15 feet (non-utility-compatible) to 25 feet (large trees).\n\n2. **Large Trees in Restricted Zones**: Cedar Elm (10 proposed; 50-60 ft mature) and Live Oak (2 proposed; 50-80 ft mature) are proposed directly adjacent to the OHE line, violating the 25-foot clearance requirement for large trees.\n\n3. **No Austin Energy Approval**: No written approval from Austin Energy authorizing trees within restricted clearance zones was found in project documents. Austin Energy review of tree/landscape clearances is listed as \"Pending\" in the Master Comment Report.\n\n4. **Incomplete Clearance Documentation**: The landscape plan shows no dimension callouts, distance notes, or measurement methodology documentation for overhead electric clearances.\n\n### Findings Provided\n\n- **3 FAIL items** (EL-2.1, EL-2.3, EL-2.15): Tree placement violations\n- **6 NOT-VERIFIABLE items** (EL-2.6, EL-2.7, EL-2.8, EL-2.9, EL-2.11, EL-2.12, EL-2.13): Required information missing from plans\n\nAll findings have been documented with specific code citations, applicable areas, sheet references, and detailed deficiency descriptions as required.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"6fe0ce48-478a-49fe-85e9-e6a74537d9e2","total_cost_usd":0.27110965,"usage":{"input_tokens":7491,"cache_creation_input_tokens":103857,"cache_read_input_tokens":671974,"output_tokens":13320,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":103857},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":7491,"outputTokens":13320,"cacheReadInputTokens":671974,"cacheCreationInputTokens":103857,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.27110965,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"grouping":"2","findings":[{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.1","status":"fail","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary near trees proposed in clearance-restricted zone"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Proposed trees measured at 0.5 feet and 4 feet from overhead electric distribution conductor (southern boundary). Non-utility-compatible trees must maintain 15-foot lateral clearance from distribution conductors. Cedar Elm and Live Oak, among the proposed species, are not typically utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F criteria. Clearance violations are evident from measured distances alone."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.3","status":"fail","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary near trees proposed in clearance-restricted zone"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (Cedar Elm ~50-60 ft, Live Oak ~50-80 ft mature height—both exceeding the 40-foot threshold per ECM Appendix F) are proposed at 0.5 feet and 4 feet from the overhead electric distribution conductor. Large trees require 25-foot lateral clearance from distribution conductors. Violations confirmed by field measurements."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.6","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern, western, and eastern property boundaries where overhead electric poles are shown"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Utility poles associated with overhead electric lines are shown on the electrical design plan (Sheet 21) at multiple locations on the site perimeter. Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of utility poles are prohibited. Although poles are depicted on the electrical plan, specific locations and distances from proposed trees cannot be verified from the landscape plan without explicit dimension callouts."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.7","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Areas near Buildings 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, and 7 where transformer pads are shown"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Electrical transformer pads are located near multiple buildings on the site (per electrical plan, Sheet 21). Trees proposed within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment or between access doors and drivable surfaces are prohibited. Specific pad locations and tree positions relative to pad access doors cannot be verified without explicit dimension callouts on the landscape plan."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.8","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Areas where pull boxes, electrical meters, and underground electrical vaults are proposed"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Multiple electrical pull boxes, vaults, and underground utilities are shown on the electrical design plan. Trees proposed within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment are prohibited. Specific equipment locations and distances from proposed trees cannot be verified from the landscape plan without explicit dimension callouts."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.9","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Areas where trees are proposed within 20 feet of underground electrical equipment"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":21}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No root barrier specifications, details, or notes are evident on the landscape plan (Sheet 31) or landscape details sheet (Sheet 37). Trees proposed within 20 lateral feet of underground electric equipment require root barriers minimum 4 feet deep, positioned 5 feet from equipment. Absence of these details prevents verification of compliance."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.11","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["Reviewer Convention"],"applicableAreas":["Areas near overhead electric facilities where large trees (40+ ft mature height) are proposed"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"Large trees (Cedar Elm ~50-60 ft, Live Oak ~50-80 ft mature heights) are proposed on the site but are not visually differentiated on the landscape plan itself. While plant schedules (Sheet 35) contain mature height data, this information is not called out or symbolized on the landscape plan drawing to allow field personnel to readily identify which plantings are large trees subject to the 25-foot clearance requirement from distribution conductors."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.12","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where overhead electric line is shown"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No dimension lines, distance callouts, or measurement notes are shown on the landscape plan indicating how clearances from the overhead electric conductor were determined. Clearances must be measured from the outer (outside) conductor position, not from the utility pole centerline. Without documented measurement methodology on the plans, compliance cannot be verified."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.13","status":"not-verifiable","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where overhead electric line is shown"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31}],"documentReferences":[],"comment":"No dimension lines or measurement callouts on the landscape plan indicate the measurement reference point (tree trunk center versus canopy edge). All clearance measurements must originate from the tree trunk (planting location), not from the projected canopy edge. Without explicit documentation of measurement methodology, compliance cannot be verified."},{"deficiencyId":"EL-2.15","status":"fail","codeCitations":["UCM § 1.10.10.4"],"applicableAreas":["Southern property boundary where trees are proposed within restricted clearance zones from overhead electric conductor"],"sheetReferences":[{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":31},{"documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNumber":35}],"documentReferences":[{"documentId":"ca29fbe6-6361-4580-848e-7807ae462499","label":"Master Comment Report — Electric Review"}],"comment":"Trees are proposed at 0.5 feet and 4 feet from the overhead electric distribution conductor, within the prohibited clearance zones (15 feet for non-utility-compatible trees, 25 feet for large trees). No documented written approval from Austin Energy authorizing these plantings in restricted zones was found in the supplementary documents. The Master Comment Report identifies Austin Energy tree/landscape clearance requirements (UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4) as pending review."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"2dae5009-93a9-4d2d-9f52-abac91131c82"}
{"level":30,"time":1776980214078,"pid":122,"hostname":"0067a724-a36","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01GxsjhpCDt4EVMwvfZefwzD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The vision analysis shows that the landscape plan (Sheet 31, described as \"Sheet 28 of 35\" in the vision output) does not provide direct species identification for the trees along the southern boundary. However, I know from reading Sheet 35 that it contains the plant list with specific species information. Let me check that plant list again to see what species are being planted as mitigation trees.\n\nFrom Sheet 35, I saw in the plant list:\n- 10 Cedar Elm (100 gal., 4\" Cal.)\n- 2 Live Oak (100 gal., 4\" Cal.)\n- 2 Texas Mountain Laurel (45 gal., 2.5\" Cal.)\n- 6 Mexican Redbud (45 gal., 2.5\" Cal.)\n\nThe grouping file mentions that I need to check ECM Appendix F for:\n1. Utility-compatible designation (UC column)\n2. Mature height classifications\n\nLet me search for information about these tree species' utility-compatible status and mature heights. I should look at the grouping file again for the reference to ECM Appendix F.\n\nFrom the regulatory overview in the grouping:\n- Utility-compatible trees are marked with \"x\" in ECM Appendix F UC column\n- Large trees are those with mature height ≥ 40 feet (per ECM Appendix F for electrical purposes)\n\nLet me look up these species:\n1. Cedar Elm (Ulmus crassifolia) - Need to check if UC and mature height\n2. Live Oak (Quercus virginiana) - Need to check if UC and mature height  \n3. Texas Mountain Laurel (Sophora secundiflora) - Need to check\n4. Mexican Redbud (Cercis canadensis var. mexicana) - Need to check\n\nFrom the grouping text, I know:\n- Goldenball Lead-Tree at 25 ft is listed as UC exception\n\n\n- Texas Mountain Laurel at 25 ft is listed as UC exception. The 40-foot threshold for \"large trees\" applies specifically for electrical utility purposes, which differs from the general ECM definition. Since the plant schedule specifies minimum heights like \"100 GAL., 4\" CAL., 16' HT. MINIMUM\" for Cedar Elm, these initial plantings fall well below the 40-foot large tree threshold, meaning they won't conflict with utility lines at maturity.\n\nThe critical problem isn't the species selection—it's the placement distance. Trees positioned 0-2.2 feet from the overhead electrical line violate the 15-foot minimum clearance requirement for distribution conductors, regardless of whether they're utility-compatible. Even utility-compatible species don't justify such proximity to the line. The checklist requires evaluating whether non-utility-compatible trees exist within 15 feet of distribution conductors, but here the violation is the distance itself, not just the tree type. be additional restrictions. The 10-foot prohibition around utility poles applies regardless of tree type, and there's a 5-foot minimum from underground equipment. So even utility-compatible trees can't be placed within 10 feet of a pole or 5 feet of underground lines. Given the site location at 0-2.2 feet from the distribution conductor, this clearly violates both the zone requirements and the minimum clearance rules for poles and underground equipment. The electrical plan shows the OHE line, but it's unclear if the landscape plan adequately depicts its position relative to the trees. Looking at EL-2.10, the plant schedule appears on Sheet 35 rather than Sheet 31, which could constitute a deficiency if there's no cross-reference on the landscape plan itself. For EL-2.11, the mitigation trees don't appear to differentiate large trees (40+ ft mature height) from others—they're all marked uniformly as 'M' without height distinction. EL-2.12 appears compliant.\n\nChecking the remaining items, there are no dimension lines on the landscape plan measuring clearances from pole centerlines or canopy edges, so EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 may not be applicable. However, EL-2.14 shows the OHE line and trees on the same plan, which technically satisfies the requirement to show facilities in relation to each other, though the proximity itself presents a separate concern.\n\nFor EL-2.1, trees are positioned 0-2.2 feet from the OHE line, which fails the 15-foot requirement for non-utility-compatible species regardless of compatibility designation.\n\nEL-2.2 depends on whether transmission facilities exist on the site. The plans don't specify voltage, and typical residential installations use distribution voltage (12.47 kV or 34.5 kV) rather than transmission (69 kV+), with no evidence of transmission easements or larger structures visible. This item is likely not applicable unless voltage information becomes available.\n\nFor EL-2.3, I need to identify which trees qualify as \"large\" based on mature height of 40 feet or greater. The Cedar Elm specifications indicate a 16-foot minimum at planting, but mature height requires verification. The grouping notes show Texas Mountain Laurel as a utility-compatible exception at 25 feet mature height, which wouldn't qualify as large.\n\nEL-2.4 and EL-2.5 both hinge on transmission presence, which appears absent from the site based on available documentation. EL-2.6 is more concerning—the overhead line clearly shows pole locations marked with small \"x\" symbols, and the trees positioned at 0-2.2 feet from the line almost certainly fall within the required 10-foot clearance from poles. The transformer pads shown on the electrical plan also need evaluation for EL-2.7 compliance, particularly regarding the southern pad's proximity to planted trees.\n\nUnderground infrastructure presents additional compliance issues. Pull boxes and underground lines along the southern boundary mean trees in that area likely violate the 5-foot clearance requirement under EL-2.8. For EL-2.9, if trees are within 20 feet of this underground equipment, root barriers become mandatory, but the landscape documentation doesn't specify these details. The plant schedule referenced on Sheet 35 should be cross-checked against the landscape plan itself, as the current plan lacks a complete species identification—a potential deficiency under EL-2.10. Similarly, there's no visible differentiation for mature height categories across the plantings, which could trigger EL-2.11 issues.\n\nRegarding clearance measurements, the landscape plan contains no dimension lines, making EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 not applicable. Trees and overhead equipment are both depicted on the plan, so EL-2.14 technically passes, though the proximity violations identified earlier represent separate deficiencies. The core failures remain the same: trees at 0-2.2 feet from OHE violate the 15-foot minimum clearance, and these same trees almost certainly fall within 10 feet of utility lines as well. For EL-2.10, the species are documented on Sheet 35 but aren't cross-referenced to the landscape plan itself, making this ambiguous—it could pass or fail depending on how strictly the requirement is interpreted. EL-2.11 requires verifying large tree maturity heights against ECM Appendix F since they're not shown on the landscape plan. EL-2.12 and EL-2.13 don't apply as there are no dimension lines provided. EL-2.14 passes since both trees and overhead equipment are clearly depicted.\n\nLooking more closely at EL-2.10, the plant schedule identifies species on Sheet 35, but the landscape plan legend only uses abbreviations (M, SY, PL, P) without spelling out what those species actually are. This creates a gap between where the species information exists and where it's needed for verification on the main plan.\n\nFor EL-2.11, I need to determine if any proposed trees exceed 40 feet at maturity. The Cedar Elm specification shows 16 feet as the minimum planting height, but that's not the mature height—I need to cross-check against ECM Appendix F to see what the full mature height would be and whether it triggers the large tree differentiation requirement. planting large trees like Cedar Elm and Live Oak near overhead electric lines at 0-2.2 ft violates the clearance requirement in EL-2.3. The plan also fails to differentiate tree species by size category, which means EL-2.11 isn't satisfied.\n\nFor EL-2.9, those same southern boundary trees fall within 20 ft of underground electric equipment running along that line, so they require root barriers at minimum 4 ft deep positioned 5 ft from the equipment. The landscape plan doesn't indicate root barrier details for these trees, making this requirement either unverifiable or non-compliant depending on whether specifications appear elsewhere.\n\nI need to review the landscape details sheet to confirm whether root barrier specifications are documented there.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1701,"cache_read_input_tokens":63545,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1701,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f27eabf-4e7e-4d33-b3eb-83a00ab44689","uuid":"aaa7c01f-08b8-4e3f-b5f8-65730e7962fb"}
